From: Sébastien Taylor <sebastien@au-zone.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] hw_bringup: verify uiodma PCI bind, not just module
 residency

handle_uiodma_arm() only checks whether the uiodma kernel module is
resident (lsmod) before deciding whether to bind it to the Ara-2 PCIe
device via the "new_id" sysfs write. That write only happens the first
time the module is modprobed in a given boot.

rt-sdk-ara2.service runs this script with Restart=always. If an early
attempt binds the device then loses the binding (e.g. an aborted DDR
bringup tears down PCI device state), uiodma stays loaded but unbound.
Every subsequent restart within that boot sees "module already
loaded", skips the bind step entirely, and kinara_main immediately
fails with "uiodma file path doesnt exist" -- the crash loop can never
self-heal until the next power cycle reloads the module fresh.

Verify the actual driver binding for the Ara-2 PCI device on every
run, independent of module residency, and (re)establish it if needed.

Upstream-Status: Pending [reported to NXP]
Signed-off-by: Sébastien Taylor <sebastien@au-zone.com>
---
 usr/share/rt-sdk-ara240_2.1.1/scripts/hw_bringup.sh | 22 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/usr/share/rt-sdk-ara240_2.1.1/scripts/hw_bringup.sh
+++ b/usr/share/rt-sdk-ara240_2.1.1/scripts/hw_bringup.sh
@@ -70,9 +70,29 @@
         log_message "$GREEN" "UIODMA already loaded."
     else
         modprobe uiodma
-        echo "1e58 0002" > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/uiodma/new_id
         log_message "$GREEN" "UIODMA module inserted."
     fi
+
+    # EDGEFIRST: module residency alone does not mean the PCI device is
+    # bound to uiodma. Restart=always reruns this whole script within the
+    # same boot without unloading uiodma; if an earlier attempt bound the
+    # device then lost it (e.g. an aborted DDR bringup), the check above
+    # skips rebinding and every later retry fails immediately with
+    # "uiodma file path doesnt exist" for the rest of that boot. Verify
+    # the actual bind and (re)establish it regardless of module state.
+    for pci_dev in /sys/bus/pci/devices/*; do
+        [ -f "$pci_dev/vendor" ] || continue
+        grep -q "0x1e58" "$pci_dev/vendor" 2>/dev/null || continue
+        dev_id=$(basename "$pci_dev")
+        if [ "$(basename "$(readlink -f "$pci_dev/driver" 2>/dev/null)" 2>/dev/null)" = "uiodma" ]; then
+            log_message "$GREEN" "PCI device $dev_id already bound to uiodma."
+        else
+            log_message "$YELLOW" "PCI device $dev_id not bound to uiodma, rebinding..."
+            echo "$dev_id" > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/uiodma/bind 2>/dev/null \
+                || echo "1e58 0002" > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/uiodma/new_id 2>/dev/null \
+                || true
+        fi
+    done
 }
 
